    "items": [
      {
        "text": "Laura Friedman is the U.S. Representative for California's 30th District. She serves on the House Committees on Transportation and Infrastructure and Science, Space, and Technology. Before entering politics, she worked as a film and television producer. She previously represented the 44th District in the California State Assembly and served on the Glendale City Council, including a term as mayor.",
        "cites": [1],
        "sentences": [
          {
            "text": "Laura Friedman is the U.S. Representative for California's 30th District.",
            "cites": [1]
          },
          {
            "text": "She serves on the House Committees on Transportation and Infrastructure and Science, Space, and Technology.",
            "cites": [1]
          },
          {
            "text": "Before entering politics, she worked as a film and television producer.",
            "cites": [1]
          },
          {
            "text": "She previously represented the 44th District in the California State Assembly and served on the Glendale City Council, including a term as mayor.",
            "cites": [1]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "She has pushed for a national film tax credit to bring production jobs back to the United States. Friedman co-convened a House housing affordability working group. She introduced the Cut Red Tape for Housing Act to reduce bureaucracy that holds up affordable housing. She introduced the Next Gen Road Safety Act to help law enforcement agencies obtain technology like GPS-equipped darts to de-escalate high-speed pursuits. She co-led legislation to guarantee automatic refunds to small businesses for tariffs later ruled illegal by the Supreme Court. She also co-led bills to unmask ICE agents and to raise the federal minimum wage. Her campaign says she supports universal healthcare and childcare.",
        "cites": [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7],
        "sentences": [
          {
            "text": "She has pushed for a national film tax credit to bring production jobs back to the United States.",
            "cites": [2]
          },
          {
            "text": "Friedman co-convened a House housing affordability working group.",
            "cites": [3]
          },
          {
            "text": "She introduced the Cut Red Tape for Housing Act to reduce bureaucracy that holds up affordable housing.",
            "cites": [4]
          },
          {
            "text": "She introduced the Next Gen Road Safety Act to help law enforcement agencies obtain technology like GPS-equipped darts to de-escalate high-speed pursuits.",
            "cites": [5]
          },
          {
            "text": "She co-led legislation to guarantee automatic refunds to small businesses for tariffs later ruled illegal by the Supreme Court.",
            "cites": [6]
          },
          {
            "text": "She also co-led bills to unmask ICE agents and to raise the federal minimum wage.",
            "cites": [4]
          },
          {
            "text": "Her campaign says she supports universal healthcare and childcare.",
            "cites": [7]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "In the Assembly, she secured more than $60 million for local projects including the Glendale Riverwalk and Griffith Park safety improvements. She authored a state law barring local parking requirements near transit. She also wrote legislation creating a speed-camera pilot program. In Congress, she secured a $1,092,000 federal investment to replace the Rose Bowl's aging water and wastewater systems. She secured $500,000 for transit planning, including $250,000 for designing shaded bus shelters. Her congressional office says it has returned more than $5.5 million to constituents by resolving 1,450 cases with agencies like the IRS and Social Security.",
        "cites": [1, 8, 9, 3, 10, 4],
